"""Current review: original body restored, converter retained, roof defect traced."""
from pathlib import Path
import base64,hashlib,json
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
receipt=json.loads((P/'original-body-converter/fusion-receipt.json').read_text())
fit=json.loads((P/'original-body-converter/converter-local-fit.json').read_text())
assert receipt['status']=='COMPLETE' and receipt['readbackPassed'] and receipt['meshBodies']==161
assert fit['staticClear'] and fit['expectedStaticPairs']==fit['completedStaticPairs']==320
assert not fit['newPrintRequiredByThisRestoration']
def image(path):return 'data:image/png;base64,'+base64.b64encode((P/path).read_bytes()).decode()
front=image('original-body-converter/original-body-readback.png');rear=image('original-body-converter/original-body-rear.png');roof=image('roof-hole-review/roof-top-comparison.png')
body='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>MicroDuck · Original body, onboard converter</title><style>*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#f3f5f2;color:#25392f;font:17px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif}main{max-width:1320px;margin:auto;padding:28px 30px 70px}h1{font-size:40px;line-height:1.15;margin:12px 0}h2{font-size:25px;margin:30px 0 12px}h3{font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px}a{color:#216447}nav{display:flex;gap:24px;flex-wrap:wrap}.eyebrow{font-size:13px;letter-spacing:2px;color:#587564}.note{background:#e3eee2;border-left:5px solid #417351;padding:16px 22px;margin:22px 0}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:16px}.card{background:white;border:1px solid #d4ddd4;border-radius:12px;padding:20px}.hero{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.55fr 1fr;gap:20px;align-items:start}img{max-width:100%;display:block;background:white;border-radius:12px}figure{margin:0}figcaption,small,footer{font-size:14px;color:#526858}summary{font-weight:650;cursor:pointer}details{padding:16px 20px;background:white;border:1px solid #d4ddd4;border-radius:8px;margin:16px 0}.flow{display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap}.flow span{background:white;border:1px solid #a7bda8;border-radius:8px;padding:12px}.flow b{align-self:center}footer{margin-top:30px}@media(max-width:850px){.hero,.grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}main{padding:20px 16px}h1{font-size:31px}}</style><main><nav><a href="LEFT-LEG.html">Assembly guide</a><a href="HEAD-EYE-REVIEW.html">Existing head review</a><a href="#roof">Roof opening finding</a></nav><p class="eyebrow">MICRODUCK · REVISED DIRECTION · 09 OCTOBER 2026</p><h1>Original body proportions.<br>Converter in the battery location.</h1><p>The central battery clamp and widened frame are withdrawn. Restore the original leg spacing and keep the front approach clear for beak pickup.</p><div class="note"><strong>Updated in Fusion and reopened successfully.</strong> The battery and C frame are absent. The existing converter support is retained; this restoration introduces no new printed geometry.</div><div class="hero"><figure><img src="'''+front+'''" alt="Fusion model with original body and leg spacing restored, no central battery"><figcaption>Current saved Fusion model, after reopening. The existing head is retained for this body restoration; its roof aperture is a separately identified unfinished interface.</figcaption></figure><article class="card"><h3>What changed</h3><p>The left and right leg groups return <strong>11 mm inward each</strong>. Their internal parts retain the same relative positions.</p><p>Hip yaw axes return to <strong>35 mm</strong> spacing; hip pitch axes return to <strong>85 mm</strong>.</p><p>The <strong>DFR1202 5 V converter</strong> remains in the original battery region with its existing insert and support. The added eleven-position fuse carrier stays removed.</p><p>Supply remains the existing external charger. There is no onboard PB1033MI clamp in this configuration.</p></article></div><details><summary>Rear view and converter position</summary><img src="'''+rear+'''" alt="Rear Fusion view with the existing regulator receiving frame at the original battery region"><p>The existing converter insert and integrated power support are prior local adaptations, not untouched community parts. They are retained here to avoid another bracket revision merely to undo C. The module model represents its published 30 × 26 × 8 mm installation envelope.</p></details>
<h2>Converter fit checked at original spacing</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Local static clearance</h3><p>All <strong>320 selected pairs</strong> have zero intersection in the checked assembly pose. The nominal module-to-insert gap is approximately <strong>0.30 mm</strong>.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Installation sequence</h3><p>Install the board in the loose insert, then install that group in the support. Both existing local approach paths passed <strong>57 sampled positions</strong>.</p><p>Prewire the board on the bench. The soldered leads are outside this geometry check.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Scope of this result</h3><p>The saved assembly contains <strong>161 source-bound mesh references</strong>. All placements were checked again after reopening.</p><p>Loaded temperature, soldered wire exits, physical fit and full-motion acceptance remain separate.</p></article></div>
<h2 id="roof">Why is there a hole in the head?</h2><p><strong>It was introduced for the Pi's USB adapter; the community head did not have this opening.</strong> It is a real subtraction in the current cap mesh, not a missing rendered face.</p><p>The earlier generator used a <strong>25 × 18 × 8 mm assumed plug envelope</strong> whose record explicitly says the vendor dimensions were unverified. A later connector position added another cut without fully retiring the old one. The present crown opening therefore includes historical cut residue; it has not been established as the minimum necessary opening for the actual adapter.</p><details><summary>Original cap versus current cap</summary><img src="'''+roof+'''" alt="Top projection comparing the uninterrupted original community cap with the added USB opening in the current cap"><p>The coloured outline is a 9 × 19 mm cutter section, not a measurement of the real USB plug. The current plug-envelope model still intersects a restored solid roof when seated, so simply changing the assembly order does not validate filling the hole. Because that envelope is assumed, its intersection also does not prove the physical adapter needs the opening.</p></details><p><strong>Roof correction remains open.</strong> The USB path carries motor communication from the Pi to the head's OpenRB; camera power/data use CSI and audio uses I2S. Direct OpenRB-to-Mac USB is already a supported setup/debug route, but the complete Mac-control/Pi-sensor runtime migration is unfinished. The body restoration does not silently change that wiring or label the roof fixed.</p>
<h2>Power arrangement</h2><div class="flow"><span>Existing wall charger<br>and USB-C lead</span><b>→</b><span>5991 PD receiver<br>external negotiated supply</span><b>→</b><span>Tether and disconnect</span><b>→</b><span>DFR1202<br>at original battery location</span><b>→</b><span>5 V distribution<br>logic and motor branches</span></div><p>The power-distribution revision continues without the added eleven-holder fuse array. Branch ratings, common ground and motor-bus ownership must be reflected together in the final wiring instructions. The existing complete cabling guide is still under that review.</p><p><a href="https://www.dfrobot.com/product-2907.html">DFR1202 published module dimensions and ratings</a> · <a href="https://emanual.robotis.com/docs/en/parts/controller/openrb-150/">OpenRB interface documentation</a></p>
<h2>Keep the useful wiring correction</h2><p>The registered left-knee sockets showed why the old order failed: a rear straight plug approach meets the orange thigh wall. Preconnecting the plugs before installing the motor passes the checked housing sweep. That local evidence remains useful because this restoration preserves the knee geometry.</p><p>The next assembly revision binds actual connectors, reachable plug approaches, cable slack and closure order to the same CAD parts. It does not infer physical wire routing from a line drawn between part centres.</p><footer>Current direction: original body + onboard converter. C remains archived as withdrawn.<br>AI assistance was used to draft.</footer></main></html>'''
(P/'DESIGN-REVIEW.html').write_text(body)
inputs=['original-body-converter/fusion-import-manifest.json','original-body-converter/fusion-receipt.json','original-body-converter/converter-local-fit.json','roof-hole-review/roof-hole-review.json','roof-hole-review/motor-bus-context.md']
(P/'review-build-receipt.json').write_text(json.dumps({'stage':'Original body restored; converter retained; roof aperture provenance established','readyForReview':True,'readyForPrint':False,'roofFixed':False,'sources':{n:hashlib.sha256((P/n).read_bytes()).hexdigest() for n in inputs},'htmlSha256':hashlib.sha256(body.encode()).hexdigest()},indent=2)+'\n')
print(json.dumps({'bytes':len(body.encode()),'parts':161,'roofFixed':False,'newPrintGeometry':False}))
